@Chapter Introduction
This package allows symbolic computations in free multiplicative conic alternative algebras $C$
over free commutative rings $k$.
It also supports computations in several structures that can be constructed from $C$ and $k$:
namely, cubic Jordan matrix algebra as well as certain Lie algebras and groups
which are graded by the root systems $G_2$ and $F_4$.

This package was originally developed for certain computations in <Cite Key="DMW26"/>,
which contains detailed descriptions of the underlying mathematical structures.
A less technical overview of the functionality of the package is available in
<Cite Key="Wiedemann26"/>.

@Section Notational conventions
Throughout this documentation, we use the following conventions:
* Conic algebras are denoted by <C>C</C> and their commutative base rings by <C>k</C>.
* Elements of a conic algebra are denoted by <C>a, b, c</C>.
* Elements of the commutative base ring of a conic algebra are denoted by <C>t, s, r</C>.
* Elements of a cubic Jordan matrix algebra are denoted by <C>cub</C>.
* Elements of the Lie algebra $L$ over a conic algebra are denoted by <C>lie</C>.
* Elements of a Brown algebra (that is, of the components $L_1$ and $L_{-1}$ of a Lie algebra $L$) are denoted by <C>brown</C>.
* Elements of $L_0$, the 0-space of $L$, are denoted by <C>l0</C>.
* Elements of <C>DD</C> (one of the components of $L_0$) are denoted by <C>ddEl</C>.
* Endomorphisms of $L$ are denoted by <C>f, g</C>.
* We denote by <C>i, j, l</C> integers in <C>[1,2,3]</C> and by <C>p</C> arbitrary integers.<P/>

@Section Multiple conic algebras in the same GAP session

Elements of distinct conic algebras are always regarded as distinct, even if they
share the same name:

@BeginExampleSession
gap> C := ConicAlgebra(3, 2, 2);; D := ConicAlgebra(3, 2, 2);;
gap> ConicAlgIndet(C, 1);
(1)*a1
gap> ConicAlgIndet(D, 1);
(1)*a1
gap> ConicAlgIndet(C, 1) = ConicAlgIndet(D, 1);
false
@EndExampleSession

However, the elements of their base rings are (quotients of) polynomials over
the integers, and will coincide if they share the same name:

@BeginExampleSession
gap> ConicTr(ConicAlgIndet(C, 1));
tr(a1)
gap> ConicTr(ConicAlgIndet(D, 1));
tr(a1)
gap> ConicTr(ConicAlgIndet(C, 1)) = ConicTr(ConicAlgIndet(D, 1));
true
@EndExampleSession

@Section For advanced users
While the main functions of the package are described in this documentation,
more functions and their documentation can be found by studying the GAP source files in
<C>CubicJordanMatrixAlg/gap</C>.
Most files are commited to building up the desired structures step by step
(similarly as this manual does),
and the precise order in which they are loaded can be read off from
<C>CubicJordanMatrixAlg/init.g</C>.
The most complicated tasks are the simplification functions in <C>simplify.g</C>
and the construction of the trace function in <C>comring.g</C>.<P/>

@Chapter Conic
@ChapterTitle Conic algebras and their base rings

Throughout this documentation, we use the term "conic algebra" to mean
"(free) multiplicative conic alternative algebra".
Internally, these algebras are represented in <C>CubicJordanMatrixAlg</C>
by free nonassociative algebras, that is, by magma rings over free magmas.
If <C>a = b</C> evaluates to <C>true</C> for two elements <C>a, b</C> of a conic algebra,
this implies that $a=b$ is indeed an identity
(in a free conic algebra, and hence in all conic algebras).
However, if <C>a = b</C> evaluates to <C>false</C>,
this does not necessarily mean that $a \ne b$, but only
that we have not proven that $a=b$.

See also Chapter <Ref Chap="Chapter_Simplify"/> for functions to prove identities in conic algebras
(and in structures built from conic algebras).

