@Chapter CJMA
@ChapterTitle Cubic Jordan matrix algebras
This section describes the functions relating to the
cubic Jordan matrix algebra $J := \text{Her}_3(C, \Gamma)$ that is defined over
any conic algebra $C$. Throughout this section, we denote the conic algebra
over which we work by $C$ and its base ring by $k$.
The addition $J \times J \to J$ and the scalar multiplication
$k \times J \to J$ are available as
<C>+</C> and <C>*</C>.

@Section CJMA-Elements
@SectionTitle Constructing elements of cubic Jordan matrix algebras

@Section CJMA-CNS
@SectionTitle The cubic norm structure
A cubic Jordan matrix algebra $J$ has the structure of a cubic norm structure,
whose structural maps can be computed as follows.<P/>

@Section CJMA-Jordan
@SectionTitle The Jordan algebra structure
A cubic Jordan matrix algebra $J$ has the structure of a quadratic Jordan algebra,
whose structural maps can be computed as follows.<P/>

@Chapter Lie
@ChapterTitle The Lie algebra

@Section Lie-5Grad
@SectionTitle The 5-grading of the Lie algebra

The Lie algebra $L$ over any conic algebra $C$ has a direct sum decomposition
$\bigoplus_{i=-2}^2 L_i$.
As usual in GAP, the sum and Lie bracket of two elements of $L$ can be computed
as <C>lie1 + lie2</C> and <C>lie1 * lie2</C>, respectively.
Further, the scalar multiplication $k \times L \to L$, where $k$ is the base ring of $C$,
can also be computed using <C>*</C>.

@Section Lie-Brown
@SectionTitle The Brown algebra and its elements

We refer to the $k$-module $B = (k, J, J', k)$ as the <E>Brown algebra</E> because,
if $2$ and $3$ are invertible in $k$, then it has the structure of a structurable algebra
that is known under this name.
It parametrises the $1$- and $(-1)$-parts of the Lie algebra that is constructed in <Cite Key="DMW26"/>.<P/>
Note that while elements of $B$ are internally represented and also printed as
lists <C>[t, cub1, cub2, s]</C>, they are not lists in the GAP sense.
Thus for an element <C>brown</C> of $B$, we have <C>IsList(brown) = false</C> and
<C>brown[1]</C> is not a well-defined expression.
Further, we also distinguish between the element <C>[t, cub1, cub2, s]</C>
of the Brown algebra and the corresponding elements <C>[t, cub1, cub2, s]_+</C>
and <C>[t, cub1, cub2, s]_-</C> of $L_1$ and $L_{-1}$, respectively.

@Section Lie-2
@SectionTitle The parts $L_{-2}$ and $L_2$

@Section Lie-1
@SectionTitle The parts $L_{-1}$ and $L_1$
The $k$-submodules $L_{-1}$ and $L_1$ are isomorphic to the Brown algebra $B$.
However, as explained previously, we strictly distinguish between elements of
$B$, $L_{-1}$ and $L_1$.

@Section Lie-0
@SectionTitle The Lie subalgebra $L_0$
The subalgebra $L_0$ of $L$ is itself the direct sum of three components $L_{0,-1}, Z, L_{0,1}$.
We internally distinguish between elements of $L_0$ and their embedding into $L$.<P/>

The $k$-submodules $L_{0,-1}$ and $L_{0,1}$ are isomorphic to the cubic Jordan matrix algebra $J$.
Again, we strictly distinguish between elements of $J$, $L_{0,-1}$ and $L_{0,1}$.
For an element <C>cub</C> of $J$, the corresponding elements of $L_{0,-1}$ and $L_{0,1}$
are printed as <C>ad_{cub}^-</C> and <C>ad_{cub}^+</C>, respectively.
While we internally distinguish between elements of $L_{0,\pm 1}$ and their embeddings into $L$,
they are printed in the same way.<P/>


We refer to the $k$-module $\langle \mathbf{d}_{a,b} \mid a \in J, b \in J' \rangle$
(which is unnamed in <Cite Key="DMW26"/>) as <C>DD</C> in (the documentation of) this GAP package.
The $k$-module $Z$ is the (not necessarily direct) sum of <C>DD</C> with $k \xi + k\zeta$.
Again, we internally distinguish between elements of <C>DD</C> and their embedding into $L_0$ and $L$.<P/>

@Section Lie-Root
@SectionTitle Root homomorphisms in the Lie algebra
We represent a root in $G_2$ (respectively, $F_4$) as a list with two (respectively, four) elements,
each of which is an integer in the range <C>[-2, -1, 0, 1, 2]</C>.
This representation corresponds precisely to Figures 1 and 3 in <Cite Key="DMW26"/>.

@Section Lie-Auto
@SectionTitle Automorphisms of the Lie algebra
The $F_4$-graded group constructed in <Cite Key="DMW26"/> is a group of automorphisms of $L$.
This package provides the internal structure <C>LieEndo</C> to perform computations with
endomorphisms of $L$. <P/>
For an element <C>f</C> of <C>LieEndo</C> and <C>lie</C> in $L$,
the image of <C>lie</C> under <C>f</C> can be computed as <C>f(lie)</C>.
For two elements <C>f</C> and <C>g</C> of <C>LieEndo</C>,
<C>f+g</C> and <C>f*g</C> are the elements of <C>LieEndo</C> that map <C>lie</C>
to <C>f(lie)+g(lie)</C> and <C>f(g(lie))</C>, respectively.

@Chapter Simplify
@ChapterTitle Simplification and equality tests

@Section Simplify-Function
@SectionTitle Simplification functions
Let <C>u, v</C> be two elements of $k$ (or of $C$, the Brown algebra, <C>DD</C>, $L_0$ or $L$,
but not of <C>LieEndo</C> which is handled somewhat differently).
Then <C>u = v</C> evaluates to <C>true</C> if and only if the internal representations
of <C>u</C> and <C>v</C> coincide.
In particular, if it evaluates to <C>true</C>, then <C>u</C> and <C>v</C> are certainly equal
(in the mathematical sense, i.e. regarded as elements of a "free" mathematical structure).
If for example <C>u</C>, <C>v</C> lie in $C$ and <C>u = v</C>,
this means that for any multiplicative conic alternative algebra $\tilde{C}$
over any commutative ring $\tilde{k}$, the identity <C>u = v</C> holds whenever
<C>a1, a2, ...</C> are replaced by arbitrary elements of $\tilde{C}$ and
<C>t1, t2, ...</C> are replaced by arbitrary elements of $\tilde{k}$.<P/>
However, it <C>u = v</C> evaluates to <C>false</C>, then this does not prove that <C>u</C> and <C>v</C>
are not equal (in the mathematical sense).
The following key function of this package aims to mitigate this problem.

@Section Simplify-Test
@SectionTitle Testing equality of automorphism of $L$
For elements of <C>LieEndo</C>, equality tests with <C>=</C> are useless: Even <C>GrpRootHomF4(F4Root, z) = GrpRootHomF4(F4Root, z)</C> evaluates to <C>false</C> for technical reasons. Instead, the following functions should be used.<P/>

